  "prompt_text": "The figure compares three visible classes of cell-surface galectin assemblies and the network patterns they form among glycosylated receptors on a membrane. In each case, cartoon receptors are embedded in a lipid bilayer, and galectin-like crescent shapes bridge receptor glycans to create larger extracellular lattices.\n\nThe top row shows a “prototype” arrangement, associated with several GAL genes, in which paired blue binding units connect receptor-linked sugars into a relatively regular, grid-like network. This suggests a more ordered crosslinking geometry.\n\nThe middle row presents a “chimera-type” form, labeled GAL3. Here a red crescent domain is attached to a darker multimerizing core, producing clustered star-like assemblies. On the membrane and in the schematic network, this leads to a more irregular, branched mesh rather than a neat lattice.\n\nThe bottom row shows a “tandem repeat-type” class, linked to several other GAL genes, where two different crescent domains are joined in one molecule. These purple and gold units connect receptors in alternating orientations, generating another extended network that appears less symmetric than the prototype but more structured than the chimera-type.\n\nOverall, the figure emphasizes that different galectin architectures drive distinct receptor crosslinking patterns at the cell surface.",
